  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Parsons Government Services obligated $661M in federal contracts in FY2026 to date across 139 awards, with an average award size of $4.8M. Obligations fell 41.6% year over year. Parsons Government Services received the largest shares from the Department of Defense and the General Services Administration, which obligated $313M and $262M respectively. Parsons Government Services won most of its FY2026 business in Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology), which accounted for $299M, and in Engineering Services, which accounted for $220M. Computer Systems Design Services provided $78.3M of obligations. Historical obligations show multi-year scale: FY21 $1.1B, FY22 $1.1B, FY23 $1B, FY24 $1.3B, FY25 $1.1B, and FY26 $661M. The current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date, and some award entries in the largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables represent total contract values rather than current-year obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Parsons Government Services' federal business trended year over year?",
        "body": "Parsons Government Services obligated $661M in FY2026 to date, down 41.6% year over year. Parsons Government Services peaked in this multi-year series at $1.3B in FY24 and previously recorded $1.1B in FY21, $1.1B in FY22, $1B in FY23, and $1.1B in FY25. The current FY26 total is a fiscal year to date partial figure."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Parsons Government Services' largest federal customers and markets?",
        "body": "Department of Defense obligated $313M and General Services Administration obligated $262M in FY2026 to date. Parsons Government Services' largest markets are Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $299M and Engineering Services at $220M. Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $78.3M, and Other Scientific and Technical Consulting Services accounted for $39.3M."
      }
    ]
  },
